<dict>
	<key>bundleid</key>
	<string>com.anokhin.kobolib</string>
	<key>name</key>
	<string>Kobo Library</string>
	<key>description</key>
	<string>Search the ebook library by metadata, with covers</string>
	<key>createdby</key>
	<string>Volodymyr Anokhin</string>
	<key>category</key>
	<string>Productivity</string>
	<key>readme</key>
	<string>kb &lt;words&gt; searches the library: every word must match, by prefix, one of title, authors, series, folder, path, genre, format, language or year (kb delany epub 1975). Empty kb lists the newest books after a reminder of books without a genre.
A first word that names a command adds its rows above the books matching all the words: kb rnd, kb inbox, kb classify, kb dups, kb stats, kb fix, kb trash, kb src, kb update. Typing two letters of a command offers to complete it.
↩ does a list's main thing, ⌥↩ reveals in Finder, ⇧↩ sets the genre of a book. A list with several books starts with a head row (Import all, Set genre for all, Fix all, Trash all) that does it for every row shown.
kb classify lists the inbox; ↩ picks a genre and moves the book to its genre home at once. In the genre picker ⇧↩ creates the typed text as a new genre.
kb fix shows what is wrong and how to fix it, with Undo for the last batch; kb trash sets unfinished downloads and other books aside in _trash/; nothing is ever deleted.
kb src searches other sources (a Calibre library, a downloads folder) and ↩ copies the book into the library inbox.
Set the library root (and optionally other sources and the index folder) in the workflow configuration, then run kb update once; rerun after adding books. Needs only macOS's /usr/bin/python3.</string>
